<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  windows-auto-build-pipeline: offline-dropped unattend.xml for the specialize/oobeSystem
  passes only. Windows Server 2019 variant of unattend-server2022.xml - same proven structure,
  only the driver path (2k19 instead of 2k22) and ComputerName differ. Unlike the 2022/2025/
  Windows 11 templates (each transcribed from a live PHASE2_ENGINEERING_LOG.md bring-up
  session), this file was produced during Phase B design work
  (WINDOWS_SERVER_2019_IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md), not a live session - log filenames below use a
  "server2019" prefix rather than a fabricated "sessionNN" number for that reason. See
  unattend-server2025.xml's own header comment for the full rationale behind this file's
  structure (why PnpCustomizationsNonWinPE isn't used, why pnputil runs live via
  FirstLogonCommands instead, etc.) - not repeated here.

  Order 4-8 (WinRM enable) is split into five separate SynchronousCommand entries, one operation
  each - NOT combined into one script, and this split is the actual fix, not a stylistic choice
  (PHASE3_ENGINEERING_LOG.md, 2026-09-02 Phase E1 - the full bisection trail lives there, this is
  the short version). The original single combined script hung on Server 2019 specifically; ~15
  diagnostic boots via dev/run-server2019-specialize-test.sh isolated the actual mechanism:

    - Not Enable-PSRemoting (removed first, on suspicion of its Register-PSSessionConfiguration
      step being a documented WinRM StopPending trigger, PowerShell/JEA GitHub issue #30 - this
      project's own pywinrm usage, run_cmd/run_ps, never needed it anyway, confirmed by reading
      pywinrm's own source: it's just the plain WinRS shell resource, never native PSRP/PSSession).
      Removing it alone did NOT fix the hang.
    - Not Restart-Service WinRM, not the WSMan:\localhost\Listener remove/recreate pipe - both
      also removed and tested in isolation, neither was the cause either.
    - The actual mechanism: ANY single WSMan-config-changing operation (`winrm set ...` external
      calls AND native `Set-Item WSMan:\...` cmdlets both, individually confirmed) completes fine
      on its own - but TWO OR MORE such operations run within the SAME powershell.exe process/
      script hang on the second one, every time, regardless of which specific settings or which
      mechanism (external tool vs. native cmdlet). Some resource/lock the first WSMan config
      change takes isn't released before a second one in the same process tries to touch it again.
    - Fix: split each WSMan-config change into its own SynchronousCommand, so each gets a fresh
      powershell.exe process (already proven safe - FirstLogonCommands' own queue advances
      correctly between orders, confirmed via trivial cmd.exe stub commands early in this
      investigation) - whatever lock is held is released when that process exits, before the next
      one starts. This is the structural fix, not a Start-Sleep-between-calls workaround (a
      probabilistic alternative that was considered and deliberately not used).

  Order 3's network-category step (Order 3) deliberately has NO wait-loop, for a related but
  separate reason found in the same investigation: `Get-NetIPAddress | Where-Object {...}` hangs
  indefinitely via FirstLogonCommands on Server 2019 specifically (Get-NetIPAddress alone works
  fine; Get/Set-NetConnectionProfile both work fine; the pipe into Where-Object is what hangs).
  DHCP is fast/reliable here (confirmed directly via ipconfig), and the firewall rule above has no
  -Profile restriction, so the wait was defensive, never load-bearing - removing it entirely was
  lower-risk than working around the hanging pipe.

  NOTE for future editors of this file: keep every <Description> here terse (one short clause) - a
  verbose Description caused Windows Setup's own answer-file parser to reject the entire file as
  invalid ("Windows could not parse or process the unattend answer file... for pass [specialize]"),
  a failure mode this project hit twice before catching it - xmllint's well-formedness check does
  NOT catch this, only Windows Setup's own stricter schema validation does.
-->
<unattend xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:unattend">
  <settings pass="specialize">
    <component name="Microsoft-Windows-Shell-Setup" processorArchitecture="amd64" publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35" language="neutral" versionScope="nonSxS" xmlns:wcm="http://schemas.microsoft.com/WMIConfig/2002/State" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance">
      <ComputerName>WIN2019-DESIGN</ComputerName>
      <TimeZone>UTC</TimeZone>
      <RegisteredOwner>Windows Lab</RegisteredOwner>
    </component>
  </settings>

  <settings pass="oobeSystem">
    <component name="Microsoft-Windows-Shell-Setup" processorArchitecture="amd64" publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35" language="neutral" versionScope="nonSxS" xmlns:wcm="http://schemas.microsoft.com/WMIConfig/2002/State" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance">
      <OOBE>
        <HideEULAPage>true</HideEULAPage>
        <HideOEMRegistrationScreen>true</HideOEMRegistrationScreen>
        <HideOnlineAccountScreens>true</HideOnlineAccountScreens>
        <HideWirelessSetupInOOBE>true</HideWirelessSetupInOOBE>
        <NetworkLocation>Work</NetworkLocation>
        <ProtectYourPC>3</ProtectYourPC>
        <SkipUserOOBE>true</SkipUserOOBE>
        <SkipMachineOOBE>true</SkipMachineOOBE>
      </OOBE>
      <UserAccounts>
        <AdministratorPassword>
          <Value>TestP@ssw0rd123</Value>
          <PlainText>true</PlainText>
        </AdministratorPassword>
      </UserAccounts>
      <AutoLogon>
        <Enabled>true</Enabled>
        <LogonCount>5</LogonCount>
        <Username>Administrator</Username>
        <Password>
          <Value>TestP@ssw0rd123</Value>
          <PlainText>true</PlainText>
        </Password>
      </AutoLogon>
      <FirstLogonCommands>
        <SynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>1</Order>
          <RequiresUserInput>false</RequiresUserInput>
          <Description>Install NetKVM network driver live (pnputil) - DriverDatabase alone only PCI-matches it, not full NDIS class install; see Finding 39/40</Description>
          <CommandLine>cmd.exe /c pnputil /add-driver C:\Drivers\NetKVM\2k19\amd64\netkvm.inf /install &gt; C:\server2019-pnputil-log.txt 2&gt;&amp;1</CommandLine>
        </SynchronousCommand>
        <SynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>2</Order>
          <RequiresUserInput>false</RequiresUserInput>
          <Description>Log progress marker</Description>
          <CommandLine>cmd.exe /c echo firstlogon-reached &gt; C:\server2019-firstlogon-marker.txt</CommandLine>
        </SynchronousCommand>
        <SynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>3</Order>
          <RequiresUserInput>false</RequiresUserInput>
          <Description>Set network category to Private (no wait-loop - see this file's own header comment)</Description>
          <CommandLine>powershell.exe -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -Command "Get-NetConnectionProfile | Set-NetConnectionProfile -NetworkCategory Private *&gt;&gt; C:\server2019-netcat-log.txt"</CommandLine>
        </SynchronousCommand>
        <SynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>4</Order>
          <RequiresUserInput>false</RequiresUserInput>
          <Description>WinRM: startup type Automatic (split across Order 4-8 - see header comment)</Description>
          <CommandLine>powershell.exe -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -Command "Set-Service WinRM -StartupType Automatic *&gt;&gt; C:\server2019-winrm-log.txt"</CommandLine>
        </SynchronousCommand>
        <SynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>5</Order>
          <RequiresUserInput>false</RequiresUserInput>
          <Description>WinRM: enable Basic auth</Description>
          <CommandLine>powershell.exe -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -Command "winrm set winrm/config/service/auth '@{Basic=\"true\"}' *&gt;&gt; C:\server2019-winrm-log.txt"</CommandLine>
        </SynchronousCommand>
        <SynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>6</Order>
          <RequiresUserInput>false</RequiresUserInput>
          <Description>WinRM: allow unencrypted traffic</Description>
          <CommandLine>powershell.exe -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -Command "winrm set winrm/config/service '@{AllowUnencrypted=\"true\"}' *&gt;&gt; C:\server2019-winrm-log.txt"</CommandLine>
        </SynchronousCommand>
        <SynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>7</Order>
          <RequiresUserInput>false</RequiresUserInput>
          <Description>WinRM: trust all hosts</Description>
          <CommandLine>powershell.exe -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -Command "winrm set winrm/config/client '@{TrustedHosts=\"*\"}' *&gt;&gt; C:\server2019-winrm-log.txt"</CommandLine>
        </SynchronousCommand>
        <SynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>8</Order>
          <RequiresUserInput>false</RequiresUserInput>
          <Description>WinRM: open firewall port 5985</Description>
          <CommandLine>powershell.exe -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -Command "New-NetFirewallRule -DisplayName 'WinRM-HTTP' -Direction Inbound -Protocol TCP -LocalPort 5985 -Action Allow -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue *&gt;&gt; C:\server2019-winrm-log.txt"</CommandLine>
        </SynchronousCommand>
      </FirstLogonCommands>
    </component>
    <component name="Microsoft-Windows-International-Core" processorArchitecture="amd64" publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35" language="neutral" versionScope="nonSxS" xmlns:wcm="http://schemas.microsoft.com/WMIConfig/2002/State" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance">
      <InputLocale>en-US</InputLocale>
      <SystemLocale>en-US</SystemLocale>
      <UILanguage>en-US</UILanguage>
      <UserLocale>en-US</UserLocale>
    </component>
  </settings>
</unattend>
